1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,540
In 2014, in an operating room in St.

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:08,840
Louis, a nerve surgeon named Susan
Mackinnon was looking for one small

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:10,410
nerve in a patient's knee.

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,770
The patient had lived with pain for
years, after several operations.

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,910
The nerve wasn't where the
textbooks said it would be.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,740
Her colleague, Andrew Yee, went to an
old anatomy atlas, photographed

7
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:29,290
one of its paintings, and sent the
picture into the operating room.

8
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:32,070
With it, they found the nerve.

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,050
The patient got better.

10
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:42,110
The atlas was made in Vienna, between
1937 and 1960, by an anatomist

11
00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:45,080
named Eduard Pernkopf and a
team of painters.

12
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,700
Many surgeons will tell you there
has never been a better one.

13
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,060
During the war, Pernkopf's institute
received the bodies of at least

14
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:59,440
thirteen hundred and seventy-seven
people executed by the Nazi regime,

15
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:02,120
most of them for political reasons.

16
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:03,950
Some of the paintings were
made in those years.

17
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:09,310
No one can say whose body
that page showed.

18
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,990
No one in any of the paintings
has ever been identified.

19
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:18,190
And the patient in St.

20
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:22,320
Louis wasn't told, at the time,
where the picture came from.

21
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,010
So, should surgeons use it?

22
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:31,740
If a book holds knowledge that came
from people who were killed, and

23
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:37,300
never agreed, and that knowledge can
heal someone today, is it right

24
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:42,130
to use it? And if it is, what do
we owe the people in it?

25
00:01:44,810 --> 00:01:47,770
We went through what the University
of Vienna's own investigation

26
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:53,830
found, what is still unknown, and
what surgeons, historians and a

27
00:01:53,850 --> 00:01:57,560
rabbi who survived
Bergen-Belsen have argued since.

28
00:01:58,530 --> 00:02:02,960
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

29
00:02:16,101 --> 00:02:20,361
Eduard Pernkopf was born in 1888, in
a village in Lower Austria.

30
00:02:20,381 --> 00:02:23,481
He studied medicine in
Vienna and never left.

31
00:02:23,501 --> 00:02:28,141
In 1933 he became a full professor and
head of one of the university's

32
00:02:28,161 --> 00:02:30,141
two anatomy institutes.

33
00:02:30,161 --> 00:02:33,601
That same year, he signed a contract
with a Vienna publisher for an

34
00:02:33,621 --> 00:02:35,111
anatomy book for his students.

35
00:02:36,431 --> 00:02:41,031
To illustrate it, he hired painters:
Erich Lepier, Ludwig Schrott,

36
00:02:41,051 --> 00:02:45,751
Karl Endtresser and Franz Batke.

37
00:02:45,771 --> 00:02:49,131
They painted from bodies dissected at
his institute, one layer at

38
00:02:49,151 --> 00:02:49,701
a time.

39
00:02:51,261 --> 00:02:53,331
The first volume came out in 1937.

40
00:02:54,061 --> 00:02:58,001
The last was finished in 1960, five
years after Pernkopf died.

41
00:02:58,021 --> 00:03:02,631
Four volumes, in seven books, with
about eight hundred paintings.

42
00:03:04,031 --> 00:03:08,151
In the 1960s, a German editor rearranged
the paintings into a two-volume

43
00:03:08,171 --> 00:03:12,851
atlas, published in English, Italian and
Japanese, and later in Spanish

44
00:03:12,871 --> 00:03:17,241
and French. For decades, it was
the atlas surgeons wanted.

45
00:03:17,931 --> 00:03:21,891
One historian of medical illustration
called it the standard by which

46
00:03:21,911 --> 00:03:23,161
all others are measured.

47
00:03:24,441 --> 00:03:26,811
But some of the paintings
carried something else.

48
00:03:27,301 --> 00:03:31,851
Next to his name, Erich Lepier
sometimes painted a swastika.

49
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:35,891
In later editions, the publisher
airbrushed the symbols out.

50
00:03:36,441 --> 00:03:37,271
It missed a few.

51
00:03:38,931 --> 00:03:41,081
So, the first weight on the scale.

52
00:03:41,671 --> 00:03:45,891
By almost every account, it's a great
book, and at least one of the

53
00:03:45,911 --> 00:03:48,141
people who made it didn't
hide what he believed.

54
00:03:48,871 --> 00:03:51,601
But a signature doesn't tell us
how the book was made.

55
00:03:52,031 --> 00:03:55,811
For that, we need to know about the
man who ran the institute, and

56
00:03:55,831 --> 00:03:56,901
the bodies it received.

57
00:04:03,938 --> 00:04:09,108
Pernkopf had joined the Nazi party in 1933,
and soon after, its paramilitary

58
00:04:09,358 --> 00:04:13,848
wing, the SA. In March 1938,
Germany annexed Austria.

59
00:04:14,578 --> 00:04:18,198
Within weeks, the university's dean of
medicine was arrested and removed,

60
00:04:18,218 --> 00:04:20,908
and Pernkopf was put in
charge of the faculty.

61
00:04:22,428 --> 00:04:27,258
In April 1938, he gave his first
address as dean, in his SA uniform.

62
00:04:28,028 --> 00:04:32,508
In it, he called for, in his own words,
the exclusion of the hereditarily

63
00:04:32,528 --> 00:04:35,378
inferior, by sterilisation
and other means.

64
00:04:37,978 --> 00:04:38,738
His faculty was purged.

65
00:04:38,758 --> 00:04:42,818
More than half of its teachers were
dismissed, most because they were

66
00:04:42,838 --> 00:04:47,278
Jewish. The most cited count is a
hundred and fifty-three out of a

67
00:04:47,298 --> 00:04:49,858
hundred and ninety-seven.

68
00:04:49,878 --> 00:04:54,378
The historian Sabine Hildebrandt holds
Pernkopf, as dean, personally

69
00:04:54,398 --> 00:04:58,618
responsible. Across the whole
university that spring, about three

70
00:04:58,638 --> 00:05:02,218
hundred and fifty teachers and more
than two thousand Jewish students

71
00:05:02,238 --> 00:05:03,168
were driven out.

72
00:05:04,668 --> 00:05:08,018
In 1943 he became rector of
the whole university.

73
00:05:08,668 --> 00:05:13,228
When the war ended in 1945, he lost
every post, and the American army

74
00:05:13,248 --> 00:05:16,858
held him in an internment camp
near Salzburg until 1947.

75
00:05:17,768 --> 00:05:19,658
He was never charged with a war crime.

76
00:05:21,018 --> 00:05:23,188
The university never gave
him his job back.

77
00:05:23,438 --> 00:05:27,418
But it let him go on working on the
atlas, as a guest, in two borrowed

78
00:05:27,438 --> 00:05:31,648
rooms. He was still working on it
when he died, in 1955.

79
00:05:34,388 --> 00:05:35,998
So, the second weight.

80
00:05:36,588 --> 00:05:40,108
This atlas wasn't made by a scientist
who just happened to live under

81
00:05:40,128 --> 00:05:44,898
the Nazis. It was made by a committed
Nazi who ran a faculty for them.

82
00:05:45,408 --> 00:05:47,098
That tells us what to think of him.

83
00:05:47,828 --> 00:05:51,288
Whether it changes what we think of
the book depends on one thing:

84
00:05:51,308 --> 00:05:53,218
whose bodies were painted.

85
00:05:59,519 --> 00:06:03,049
For forty years, almost nobody asked
where the bodies came from.

86
00:06:03,899 --> 00:06:07,409
In the 1980s, a few doctors and
historians started to.

87
00:06:08,059 --> 00:06:13,199
In 1994, Howard Israel, an oral
surgeon at Columbia University, and

88
00:06:13,219 --> 00:06:16,859
the physician and historian William
Seidelman took the question to

89
00:06:16,879 --> 00:06:19,709
Yad Vashem, Israel's
Holocaust memorial.

90
00:06:20,579 --> 00:06:25,419
In March 1995, Yad Vashem wrote to the
University of Vienna and to

91
00:06:25,439 --> 00:06:27,589
the publisher, asking for an inquiry.

92
00:06:29,079 --> 00:06:32,709
At first, the universities said no
victims could possibly be in the

93
00:06:32,729 --> 00:06:37,669
atlas. Then, in 1997, the University
of Vienna set up a commission

94
00:06:37,689 --> 00:06:41,219
to find out. It reported
in October 1998.

95
00:06:43,079 --> 00:06:44,049
Here's what it found.

96
00:06:45,159 --> 00:06:49,019
Under a decree of 1939, the bodies of
people executed in Vienna went

97
00:06:49,039 --> 00:06:50,779
to the university's
anatomy institutes.

98
00:06:50,799 --> 00:06:56,779
Between 1938 and 1945, Pernkopf's
institute received at least thirteen

99
00:06:56,799 --> 00:06:59,559
hundred and seventy-seven.

100
00:06:59,579 --> 00:07:03,219
That counts only the ones
confirmed by a death certificate.

101
00:07:03,239 --> 00:07:05,409
The commission said the real
number was higher.

102
00:07:06,649 --> 00:07:10,459
Most of them were executed at the Vienna
district court, by guillotine.

103
00:07:11,089 --> 00:07:14,459
More than half had been convicted of
resistance or high treason.

104
00:07:15,189 --> 00:07:19,079
Others were put to death for
black-marketeering, for pickpocketing,

105
00:07:19,289 --> 00:07:20,799
for listening to foreign radio.

106
00:07:21,509 --> 00:07:23,999
Eight of the executed were
identified as Jewish.

107
00:07:24,949 --> 00:07:27,279
Seven of their bodies went
to the institute.

108
00:07:29,759 --> 00:07:32,679
The commission found no evidence that
bodies from the concentration

109
00:07:32,699 --> 00:07:34,929
camps reached the Vienna institute.

110
00:07:35,879 --> 00:07:40,139
So the people in question are not, as is
often said, Holocaust victims

111
00:07:40,159 --> 00:07:45,339
as a group. They were people the
regime sentenced to death, most of

112
00:07:45,359 --> 00:07:46,589
them for opposing it.

113
00:07:47,289 --> 00:07:49,339
Then the paintings.

114
00:07:50,269 --> 00:07:54,089
The commission counted seven
hundred and ninety-one.

115
00:07:54,109 --> 00:07:57,579
Forty-one are signed with dates
between 1938 and 1945.

116
00:07:58,969 --> 00:08:02,189
For those, it concluded, the models
probably came from the executed.

117
00:08:02,209 --> 00:08:06,559
About three hundred and fifty
can't be dated at all.

118
00:08:08,619 --> 00:08:12,469
Whether any one painting shows an
executed person, the commission

119
00:08:12,839 --> 00:08:16,259
said, can neither be
confirmed nor ruled out.

120
00:08:16,279 --> 00:08:20,549
It made a list of every executed
person handed to the institute, by

121
00:08:20,959 --> 00:08:23,849
name, but it could not match a single
name to a single painting.

122
00:08:24,459 --> 00:08:28,089
In 2021, the Medical University
of Vienna went further.

123
00:08:28,679 --> 00:08:32,259
It must be assumed, it said, that a
considerable number of the paintings

124
00:08:32,279 --> 00:08:36,549
made during and after the war are based
on the bodies of these victims.

125
00:08:38,909 --> 00:08:42,269
The commission also found remains of
executed people still kept in

126
00:08:42,289 --> 00:08:48,789
the faculty's collections: nearly a
hundred specimens labelled as

127
00:08:48,809 --> 00:08:51,249
coming from the court.

128
00:08:51,269 --> 00:08:52,669
They were taken out and buried.

129
00:08:52,689 --> 00:08:54,929
The institute's own register of bodies
was thought to have been destroyed

130
00:08:54,949 --> 00:08:58,849
in an air raid in 1945.

131
00:08:58,869 --> 00:09:01,869
In 2019, most of it turned up
in the anatomy library.

132
00:09:01,889 --> 00:09:04,629
The entries for the war years
haven't been published yet.

133
00:09:04,649 --> 00:09:06,729
They may one day narrow the gap.

134
00:09:06,749 --> 00:09:08,459
They will probably never close it.

135
00:09:10,439 --> 00:09:12,689
So, the third weight,
and the heaviest.

136
00:09:13,079 --> 00:09:15,189
We can't name anyone in this atlas.

137
00:09:15,519 --> 00:09:19,119
But it is very likely that some of the
people in its pages were put

138
00:09:19,139 --> 00:09:23,239
to death by the regime and handed to the
institute that made it, without

139
00:09:23,259 --> 00:09:28,099
anyone's consent. Every surgeon who
opens it today opens it knowing

140
00:09:28,119 --> 00:09:28,629
that.

141
00:09:35,382 --> 00:09:38,642
Once the questions started, the
publisher stopped printing the atlas,

142
00:09:38,662 --> 00:09:43,462
in the mid-1990s. But the copies
stayed on library shelves, and the

143
00:09:43,482 --> 00:09:44,692
images spread online.

144
00:09:45,502 --> 00:09:50,582
In 2019, Elsevier, which now owns the
rights, gave the original paintings

145
00:09:50,602 --> 00:09:52,532
to Vienna's medical university.

146
00:09:54,092 --> 00:09:59,612
It's still in use. In a 2019 survey of
nerve surgeons, more than half

147
00:09:59,632 --> 00:10:02,692
knew the atlas, and thirteen
percent were using it.

148
00:10:02,712 --> 00:10:06,602
Among oral and facial surgeons
in 2021, nine percent.

149
00:10:09,062 --> 00:10:13,022
When the nerve surgeons were told its
history, sixty-nine percent

150
00:10:13,042 --> 00:10:15,152
said they were still
comfortable using it.

151
00:10:15,522 --> 00:10:18,782
When the authors offered conditions
for using it ethically, three

152
00:10:18,802 --> 00:10:20,232
quarters of the rest agreed.

153
00:10:21,852 --> 00:10:23,542
Medical students hardly know it.

154
00:10:24,032 --> 00:10:28,792
At one American medical school in
2017, ninety-five percent of the

155
00:10:28,812 --> 00:10:31,122
students who answered had
never heard of it.

156
00:10:31,732 --> 00:10:36,112
Shown its pages next to a modern
atlas, most found Pernkopf's more

157
00:10:36,132 --> 00:10:37,442
complete and accurate.

158
00:10:38,152 --> 00:10:41,662
But three quarters said the modern one
was more useful for learning.

159
00:10:42,292 --> 00:10:44,882
Almost all of them wanted
its history taught.

160
00:10:46,362 --> 00:10:47,942
After the operation in St.

161
00:10:47,962 --> 00:10:51,442
Louis, Mackinnon and Yee asked
whether they had been right to use

162
00:10:51,462 --> 00:10:56,422
it. The question went to Rabbi Joseph
Polak, the head of a rabbinical

163
00:10:56,442 --> 00:11:00,892
court in Massachusetts, who as a small
child survived Bergen-Belsen.

164
00:11:01,942 --> 00:11:04,152
In 2017 he answered.

165
00:11:04,882 --> 00:11:08,832
Normally, he wrote, Jewish law forbids
taking benefit from the dead.

166
00:11:09,322 --> 00:11:12,502
But most authorities would certainly
permit these drawings to be used

167
00:11:12,522 --> 00:11:13,412
to save a life.

168
00:11:14,892 --> 00:11:18,652
On one condition: everyone must be
told exactly what these drawings

169
00:11:18,672 --> 00:11:23,372
are. That way, he wrote, the dead are
given at least some of the dignity

170
00:11:23,392 --> 00:11:27,052
they are owed. His ruling is now
known as the Vienna Protocol.

171
00:11:27,072 --> 00:11:31,732
It was first presented at a meeting at
Yad Vashem, but it is his ruling,

172
00:11:31,752 --> 00:11:33,022
not the memorial's.

173
00:11:35,842 --> 00:11:40,222
In 2021, Vienna's medical university,
the successor to Pernkopf's

174
00:11:40,242 --> 00:11:42,222
own faculty, gave its answer.

175
00:11:42,242 --> 00:11:45,952
The atlas, it said, is far
from irreplaceable.

176
00:11:46,422 --> 00:11:49,922
Its use should be cut to a minimum, and
it should preferably no longer

177
00:11:49,942 --> 00:11:50,952
be used in teaching.

178
00:11:51,392 --> 00:11:56,122
But when it really is a patient's only or
best hope, nobody will reasonably

179
00:11:56,142 --> 00:12:01,602
object. It now asks libraries to put a
note about the atlas's history

180
00:12:01,622 --> 00:12:07,302
inside every copy. Not everyone
agrees on the classroom.

181
00:12:07,322 --> 00:12:11,102
Sabine Hildebrandt once argued that
using it with its history was

182
00:12:11,122 --> 00:12:13,022
not only justifiable but desirable.

183
00:12:13,042 --> 00:12:19,482
By 2019 she had stopped using it in her
own lectures, and asks colleagues

184
00:12:19,502 --> 00:12:21,892
who do to explain where it came from.

185
00:12:24,732 --> 00:12:27,392
Mackinnon's team now
follows a set of steps.

186
00:12:27,412 --> 00:12:30,732
Stop and think. Call a colleague.

187
00:12:30,752 --> 00:12:34,742
Try another atlas. Only
then, Pernkopf.

188
00:12:34,952 --> 00:12:39,592
And Mackinnon says that now, before
she shows it to a patient, she

189
00:12:39,612 --> 00:12:42,942
tells them its history and
asks for their consent.

190
00:12:45,362 --> 00:12:47,942
Pernkopf's atlas isn't the only one.

191
00:12:47,962 --> 00:12:52,382
Some of its images were copied into
other atlases, and another German

192
00:12:52,402 --> 00:12:56,662
atlas used images linked to executed
prisoners and victims of the

193
00:12:56,682 --> 00:12:59,442
Nazi euthanasia killings.

194
00:12:59,462 --> 00:13:03,282
Scientists have also argued for decades
over whether to cite the Nazi

195
00:13:03,302 --> 00:13:06,362
freezing experiments on
prisoners at Dachau.

196
00:13:06,382 --> 00:13:09,982
But a review of those found
the data unreliable.

197
00:13:10,002 --> 00:13:14,462
The bioethicist Arthur Caplan has put
the wider point this way: not

198
00:13:14,482 --> 00:13:19,082
every use of Nazi-era information
is morally out of bounds.

199
00:13:19,102 --> 00:13:22,152
Pernkopf's paintings are very
good at what they do.

200
00:13:22,882 --> 00:13:24,832
That's what makes this case hard.

201
00:13:27,472 --> 00:13:28,742
So, the fourth weight.

202
00:13:29,412 --> 00:13:33,802
Almost everyone who has thought hard about
this, the rabbi, the university,

203
00:13:34,432 --> 00:13:37,412
the surgeons, ends up
drawing the same line.

204
00:13:37,432 --> 00:13:43,692
In the operating room, to save a life
or end real suffering, as a

205
00:13:43,712 --> 00:13:47,462
last resort, and out loud: yes.

206
00:13:48,312 --> 00:13:52,642
In the classroom, as just
another textbook: no.

207
00:13:58,821 --> 00:14:01,651
First, the strongest
case for using it.

208
00:14:02,201 --> 00:14:06,381
The people in it are already dead, and
refusing to look helps none

209
00:14:06,401 --> 00:14:11,201
of them. If a page can end a patient's
pain or save a life, choosing

210
00:14:11,221 --> 00:14:15,411
a worse outcome for a living person
isn't respect for the dead.

211
00:14:16,181 --> 00:14:18,111
It's a cost someone else pays.

212
00:14:19,001 --> 00:14:21,371
Suppressing books is what
the regime did.

213
00:14:22,021 --> 00:14:26,481
Better to use it, say where it came
from, and let it teach two lessons

214
00:14:26,501 --> 00:14:27,231
at once.

215
00:14:28,691 --> 00:14:30,701
Now the strongest case against.

216
00:14:31,371 --> 00:14:35,411
These people were killed by the state
its author served, and their

217
00:14:35,431 --> 00:14:37,801
bodies were taken without
anyone's consent.

218
00:14:38,691 --> 00:14:41,101
Every use draws benefit
from that again.

219
00:14:41,751 --> 00:14:46,441
The atlas isn't irreplaceable, and
most surgeons manage without it.

220
00:14:47,111 --> 00:14:52,351
And once you allow it to save a life,
the exception spreads: to the

221
00:14:52,371 --> 00:14:57,411
lecture hall, to the slide deck, to the
next textbook, until the history

222
00:14:57,431 --> 00:15:00,001
is gone and only the
pictures are left.

223
00:15:00,611 --> 00:15:02,661
The patient in St. Louis wasn't told.

224
00:15:03,371 --> 00:15:06,061
That is what the exception
looks like in practice.

225
00:15:07,801 --> 00:15:08,711
Here's our verdict.

226
00:15:09,581 --> 00:15:14,701
In the operating room, use it, but
only as a last resort, when it

227
00:15:14,721 --> 00:15:19,141
can save a life or end real
suffering, and tell the patient where

228
00:15:19,161 --> 00:15:23,801
it came from. Refusing would put a
living person's health below a

229
00:15:23,821 --> 00:15:25,641
gesture to the dead that
they can't receive.

230
00:15:25,661 --> 00:15:29,191
Our confidence is eighty percent.

231
00:15:29,501 --> 00:15:34,241
What would change our mind: evidence
that other atlases, or modern

232
00:15:34,261 --> 00:15:38,781
imaging, now match it in the cases
where surgeons still reach for

233
00:15:38,801 --> 00:15:44,461
it. Then it would no longer be a last
resort, and the case for using

234
00:15:44,481 --> 00:15:46,011
it would fall away.

235
00:15:49,951 --> 00:15:53,201
In the classroom, don't
teach anatomy from it.

236
00:15:53,491 --> 00:15:57,351
There are good alternatives, and a
student doesn't need these paintings

237
00:15:57,371 --> 00:15:59,201
to learn where a nerve runs.

238
00:16:00,151 --> 00:16:01,941
Teach its history instead.

239
00:16:03,031 --> 00:16:05,631
Our confidence is
seventy-five percent.

240
00:16:05,651 --> 00:16:09,251
What would change our mind: evidence
that students who learn from

241
00:16:09,271 --> 00:16:14,811
it, alongside its full history, come
away more careful doctors, not

242
00:16:14,831 --> 00:16:19,741
less. Some historians believe that, and
if the evidence backed them,

243
00:16:20,051 --> 00:16:20,721
we'd move.

244
00:16:24,781 --> 00:16:25,671
And the bigger question.

245
00:16:26,101 --> 00:16:30,351
May we use knowledge taken from people
who were killed and never agreed?

246
00:16:31,141 --> 00:16:35,041
Our verdict: we may use it to save a
life, but never in silence.

247
00:16:35,061 --> 00:16:39,281
What we owe them is the
truth, told every time.

248
00:16:39,301 --> 00:16:44,961
Our confidence is seventy percent,
lower, because the people in this

249
00:16:44,981 --> 00:16:49,171
book can't speak for themselves, and
we are deciding for them.

250
00:16:49,801 --> 00:16:54,121
What would change our mind: if the
people in it could one day be named,

251
00:16:54,141 --> 00:16:57,661
and their families asked
that it not be used.

252
00:16:57,681 --> 00:17:01,271
Their wishes should count
for more than ours.

253
00:17:05,691 --> 00:17:06,521
That's our verdict.

254
00:17:07,411 --> 00:17:10,401
It's open, which means it can change.

255
00:17:11,251 --> 00:17:14,801
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

256
00:17:15,571 --> 00:17:18,561
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

257
00:17:21,991 --> 00:17:27,311
Next case: a serial killer who was
finally caught in 2018, more than

258
00:17:27,331 --> 00:17:30,851
thirty years after his last known
attack, because distant relatives

259
00:17:30,871 --> 00:17:34,761
of his had uploaded their DNA
to a genealogy website.

260
00:17:35,111 --> 00:17:37,781
Should police be allowed to
search your family's DNA?
